print('Hello, world!');
Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program using advanced features:

```javascript
class User {
    #name;
    
    constructor(name) {
        this.#name = name;
    }

    async greet() {
        console.log(`Hello, ${this.#name}! Let's fetch a random fact.`);
        const fact = await FactAPI.getRandomFact();
        console.log(`Random Fact: ${fact}`);
    }
}

class FactAPI {
    static #apiEndpoint = "https://uselessfacts.jsph.pl/random.json?language=en";

    static async getRandomFact() {
        try {
            const response = await fetch(this.#apiEndpoint);
            if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
            const data = await response.json();
            return data.text;
        } catch (error) {
            return `Failed to fetch fact: ${error.message}`;
        }
    }
}

function* idGenerator() {
    let id = 1;
    while (true) {
        yield `User-${id++}`;
    }
}

(async () => {
    const idGen = idGenerator();
    const user1 = new User(idGen.next().value);
    const user2 = new User(idGen.next().value);

    await Promise.all([user1.greet(), user2.greet()]);
})();
```

This program demonstrates the use of classes with private fields, asynchronous functions, fetching data from an API, generators, and handling Promises.